  "query_preview": "..\n\nHigh pressure exits as rain approaches, with showers now being \nsampled via KDTX radar over western Lower Michigan. These initial \nshowers will first fill in over the Tri-Cities, before spreading \nacross the rest of Southeast Michigan this evening. This latest bout \nof rainfall is tied to the downstream effects from the phasing of a \nprogressive northern stream longwave trough and a southern stream \nlow, now opening up over the lower Missouri basin. Meanwhile, the \nresulting surface low is currently lifting across Illinois, \ndeepening as it tracks toward Lake Michigan while sustaining a broad \nregion of showers under the wider cyclonic influence. Warm/moist \nadvection is underway as a rich theta-e plume gets shoved into the \nOhio Valley, ahead of the inbound cold front. Expect a sharp uptick \nin coverage and rainfall rate after this plume engages the stronger \ndynamics, once the elevated warm nose makes further inroads into the \nregion.\n\nGiven the pace and trajectory of this low, timing issues do exist in \npairing the more robust forcing with a deeper moisture layer. Hi-Res \nmodels still depict large differences in the deformation/FGEN axis \nof the higher rainfall amounts. It is still plausible for a narrow \nstripe of 0.75+ inches of QPF to set up somewhere over the forecast \narea, generally along a southwest to northeast line. The aggregated \nNBM suite has this corridor bisecting the Tri-Cities and Thumb \nareas. Timing-wise, the peak of the event should center between the \nhours of 7 PM and 1 AM. Forecast soundings are not very bullish in \ndeveloping unstable layers, but still can't completely rule out an \nisolated rumble of thunder given upstream convective activity. \nShould also note that shower residence time ought to be higher over \nThe Thumb once the lake effect influence takes root.\n\nWith the low exiting into Lake Huron around 09Z Friday, rainfall \nshould drop off quickly once the initial cold front works through. \nThis veers winds west-southwesterly through the first half of the \nday with temperatures holding steady in the mid 50s. Additional \nshowers will be possible Friday afternoon as the secondary cold \nfront sweeps through, marked by a sharp northwesterly wind shift, \nuptick in wind/gust speeds, and brisk cold advection. Gusts in the \n35-45 mph range may arise on Friday. First, with the trailing low-\nlevel jet overnight into the morning hours during a period of higher \nstability, then again during the afternoon/evening once the mixed-\nlayer deepens from the frontal passage. The main area that could see \nborderline Wind Advisory criteria exists over The Thumb, given the \nlower friction and northerly flow off Lake Huron. The instability \ncreated by the front should also wring out lingering moisture in the \nform of rain. Any shower development associated with this activity \nshould be rather light and brief. Aggressive post-frontal drying and \ncooling then yields a clear and crisp night with many locations \nlikely to drop below freezing, more so outside of Metro Detroit. \nFrost/freeze headlines will likely be needed Friday night into \nSaturday morning with temperatures minimizing in the low 30s.\n\nDrier conditions settle in over the weekend as surface high pressure \nexpands out of the Plains. Chilly, but less windy, on Saturday with \nhighs about 10F below average, in the low-mid 50s. The surface ridge \nthen centers over Appalachia Saturday night into Sunday, leading to \na local gradient wind shift toward the southwest. This helps boost \nreadings back up to seasonal normals (low-mid 60s) by Sunday. Rain-\nfree weather likely persists through the first half of next week \nwhile split-flow situates overhead. Expect highs to run a few \ndegrees above normal next week.",
